[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Properties, Indexers, and Events in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covers properties, indexers, and events, which are essential features in C# for building robust and maintainable applications. We'll explore their purpose, syntax, and usage with practical examples."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Properties"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Properties provide a flexible mechanism to read, write, or compute the values of private fields in a class. They encapsulate access to the class's data, allowing you to control how the data is accessed and modified. This promotes data integrity and allows for validation or other logic to be executed when a field's value is accessed or changed."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class Person {
  private string _name;

  public string Name {
    get { return _name; }
    set {
      if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(value)) {
        _name = value;
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'Name' is a property that encapsulates the '_name' field. The 'get' accessor returns the value of '_name', and the 'set' accessor allows you to assign a new value to '_name', but only if the provided value is not null or empty. This demonstrates a simple validation check."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Auto-Implemented Properties"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# provides a shorthand syntax for properties called auto-implemented properties. When no additional logic is required in the 'get' or 'set' accessors, you can use this simplified syntax."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public class Product {
  public string Name { get; set; }
  public decimal Price { get; set; }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this case, the compiler automatically creates a backing field for each property. You can still access and modify these properties as you would with regular properties."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Indexers"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Indexers allow instances of a class to be accessed like arrays. They enable you to use square bracket notation ('[]') to get or set values based on an index. This is particularly useful when you want to provide a convenient way to access elements within a collection or data structure."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class StringCollection {
  private string[] _data = new string[10];

  public string this[int index] {
    get {
      if (index >= 0 && index < _data.Length) {
        return _data[index];
      }
      return null; // Or throw an exception
    }
    set {
      if (index >= 0 && index < _data.Length) {
        _data[index] = value;
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'this[int index]' is the indexer. It allows you to access elements of the '_data' array using an integer index. The 'get' accessor retrieves the element at the specified index, and the 'set' accessor assigns a value to the element at that index. The code includes a bounds check to prevent accessing elements outside the array's boundaries."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Usage example:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"StringCollection collection = new StringCollection();
collection[0] = "Hello";
string value = collection[0]; // value will be "Hello""},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Events"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Events provide a way for a class to notify other classes or objects when something of interest happens. They are based on the delegate type and follow a publisher-subscriber pattern. The class that raises the event is the publisher, and the classes that receive the notification are the subscribers."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"public class Button {
  public event EventHandler Click;

  protected virtual void OnClick(EventArgs e) {
    Click?.Invoke(this, e);
  }

  public void SimulateClick() {
    OnClick(EventArgs.Empty);
  }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'Click' is an event of type 'EventHandler'. The 'OnClick' method is a protected virtual method that raises the event. The '?.' operator is the null-conditional operator, which ensures that the event is only raised if there are any subscribers. The 'SimulateClick' method is used to trigger the event."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Subscribing to the event:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"Button button = new Button();
button.Click += (sender, e) => {
  Console.WriteLine("Button clicked!");
};

button.SimulateClick(); // Output: Button clicked!"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code creates a 'Button' instance and subscribes to its 'Click' event. When the 'SimulateClick' method is called, the 'OnClick' method raises the 'Click' event, which in turn executes the code in the subscriber (the lambda expression), printing "Button clicked!" to the console."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Custom Event Arguments"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create custom event arguments to pass specific data to the event handlers."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"public class ValueChangedEventArgs : EventArgs
{
    public int NewValue { get; set; }

    public ValueChangedEventArgs(int newValue)
    {
        NewValue = newValue;
    }
}

public class MyClass
{
    public event EventHandler<ValueChangedEventArgs> ValueChanged;

    private int _value;

    public int Value
    {
        get { return _value; }
        set
        {
            if (_value != value)
            {
                _value = value;
                OnValueChanged(new ValueChangedEventArgs(value));
            }
        }
    }

    protected virtual void OnValueChanged(ValueChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        ValueChanged?.Invoke(this, e);
    }
}"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'ValueChangedEventArgs' is a custom class derived from 'EventArgs'. It contains a 'NewValue' property that holds the new value. The 'MyClass' class has a 'ValueChanged' event that uses the 'ValueChangedEventArgs' type. When the 'Value' property is changed, the 'OnValueChanged' method is called, which raises the 'ValueChanged' event with the new value."},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding and utilizing properties, indexers, and events, you can create more modular, maintainable, and responsive C# applications."}]
